   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the environmental conservation law, in relation to the\nprohibition of hunting wild deer on state lands adjacent to a licensed\nwildlife rehabilitation facility",
    "basePrintNo": "S9345",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S9345-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §11-0901, En Con L",
    "lawSection": "Environmental Conservation Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S9345\n\nSPONSOR: PALUMBO\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the environmental conservation law, in relation to the\nprohibition of hunting wild deer on state lands adjacent to a licensed\nwildlife rehabilitation facility\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo amend provisions of law to prohibit deer hunting on state lands adja-\ncent to a wildlife rehabilitation center in the town of Southampton in\nthe county of Suffolk.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1. Amends section 11-0901 of the environmental conservation law\nto provide that deer shall not be taken in the town of Southampton,\ncounty of Suffolk on any state lands directly adjacent to the property\nwhere said county has permitted the operation of a licensed wildlife\nrehabilitation operation pursuant to section 11-0515.\n\nSection 2. Sets forth the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn January of 2021, a deer was taken by a hunter in the Henrys Hollow\nState Forest in close proximity to and in the direction of the Evelyn\nAlexander Wildlife Rescue Center. After an investigation of the incident\nby the New York State Department of Environmental Conservation, remnants\nof a discharged bullet were found to have entered a cage housing wild-\nlife in the Center's care and ultimately landed in an area where several\nemployees were working at the time, narrowly missing an individual. This\nis the most recent incident in a pattern of dangerous hunting activities\nnear the Wildlife Rescue Center since 2004 when hunting was first\npermitted in the area.\n\nDeer hunting adjacent to a wildlife rehabilitation center is an incom-\npatible use. Such a conflict also poses an existential threat to the\nsafety of the organization's employees and volunteers. It is in the\ninterest of public safety to prohibit deer hunting where such conflicts\nexist.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nMinimal\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately\n",
